if you were in an airport on october 9th  you might have seen this  long queues and i mean really long  queues  southwest airlines which has been ranked  by wsj as one of america's best  performing airlines cancelled around 1  900 flights across the us in just one  weekend the initial disruption according  to southwest started on friday night in  florida with air traffic control issues  and disruptive weather but while most of  the airlines were back up and running on  saturday morning southwest was stuck in  turmoil for days when an airline gets  behind it it's hard to catch up so what  took southwest down well the company  like other airlines has been navigating  a difficult balancing act as it tries to  recover from the impacts of the  chronovirus pandemic and while some of  the issues the company faces may be  unique to south west others are faced by  the entire us aviation sector so as we  head towards the holidays does this mean  we're likely to see more cancellations  well to get a better idea i wanted to  know exactly what took down southwest  now if there's one person who knows it's  our aviation reporter alison cider she's  been covering the aviation sector  throughout the pandemic and if you've  been following her headlines you might  have seen this coming so here's what we  know  firstly southwest went into that weekend  with staffing levels that hadn't  recovered from the pandemic like most  airlines southwest had agreed to not  furlough or lay off staff during the  pandemic in exchange for access to  billions of dollars in aid but the  company did like many other airlines  offer employees early retirement or  extended leave as it tried to reduce  staffing costs they had you know about  5000 employees up to retire and 11 000  go on some kind of leave and you know  those leaves they might have been a  couple of months they might have been a  couple years but demand for flights  returned far quicker than many in the  industry anticipated and that left  airlines with a bit of a staffing crisis  they started calling everyone back who  was on leads and though some of those  staff did come back southwest is now in  a hiring spree the company says it aims  to hire some 5 000 people this year and  some 8 000 people next year but overall  in the industry there's a lot of  competition for pilots right now you  know their training takes a really long  time so it's not like you can just pick  someone up off the street  then there were the routes that south  west were flying that weekend or rather  the sheer number of them last fall  southwest embarked on a new strategy  they had planes that they weren't using  i'm still getting paid to keep all their  employees on staff so they have a lot of  employees who are underutilized and they  started looking at you know where could  we go to pick up more revenue you know  where might there be pockets of demand  that we could access and they embarked  on this very aggressive expansion plan  they announced 18 new cities you know  over the course of the year they weren't  alone towards the end of march there are  150 new domestic routes announced by  airlines but by the summer some airlines  began trimming back the number of  planned flights to alleviate potential  strains southwest was actually one of  those airlines in august the company  said it was trimming some of its routes  in order to catch up on hiring new staff  but on that sunday october 10th the day  of the cancellations southwest actually  had more flights planned than it had in  any day since april 13th 2020 that's  right at the start of the pandemic  but part of the reason friday night's  disruption affected southwest more than  any other carrier is due to how  southwest operates  see most operators run a hub and spoke  service where planes go from and return  to a central hub while southwest does  have some hubs the company mostly runs a  point-to-point system where its jets  continue their journey on to the next  airport the net impact of this is on  that weekend the pilots that south west  did have were scattered across a range  of rooms and according to their ceo when  the system was disrupted southwest  couldn't get its planes or its pilots to  where it needed them to be the only way  for southwest to fix the problem was to  have a full reset you know sometimes  they just need to rip off the band-aid  and cancel a lot of flights in order to  get  aircraft and people back where they need  to be to sort of start fresh  so what does this mean for the flights  going into the holidays well given that  most industries are facing staffing  problems as more u.s workers have quit  their job than at any point over the  past two decades the aviation industry  is unlikely to fix its staffing problems  overnight what airlines can do however  is fly less routes less often to ensure  that they have enough staff to go around  so for consumers less choice for a  thanksgiving flight may mean you have a  better chance of actually getting there  it probably goes without saying but if  you want to know more about the aviation  sector make sure you follow alice  insider i've left a link to her coverage  below and as always if you're interested  in the future of how we might be getting  from a to b then don't forget to  subscribe catch you later  [Music]
 

